====================================================================================================
A. First-run law: L1(n) = v2(n) + v2((q-2) n + d)   for the rule (q n + d)/2,   all 0 < |n| <= 300000
   ((q-2) n + d vanishes at the fixed point -d/(q-2) of the odd branch; n vanishes at the fixed point of n/2)
====================================================================================================
  3x+1: law holds: True   excluded (branch fixed points in Z): [-1, 0]   quadratic n((q-2)n+1)/2 = T_n
  3x-1: law holds: True   excluded (branch fixed points in Z): [0, 1]   quadratic n((q-2)n-1)/2 = T_(n-1)
  5x+1: law holds: True   excluded (branch fixed points in Z): [0]   quadratic n((q-2)n+1)/2 = not triangular
  5x-1: law holds: True   excluded (branch fixed points in Z): [0]   quadratic n((q-2)n-1)/2 = not triangular
  7x+1: law holds: True   excluded (branch fixed points in Z): [0]   quadratic n((q-2)n+1)/2 = not triangular
  7x+3: law holds: True   excluded (branch fixed points in Z): [0]   quadratic n((q-2)n+3)/2 = not triangular
  9x+1: law holds: True   excluded (branch fixed points in Z): [0]   quadratic n((q-2)n+1)/2 = not triangular
  -> every rule has a first-run law; what is special to 3x+-1 is only that the odd-branch fixed point (-+1)
     is an integer, so the quadratic is n(n+-1)/2.  The law itself does not single out 3, nor the sign.

